{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Therefore. For the raisin-cakes.</b> Some say [it is derived] from a root [י-ס-ד], and the meaning is that they moan, and it is fitting for them, when they recount the excellence of Kir-Hareseth's raisin-cakes, and so in the Aramaic Targum ואושיא יחיטו (Ezra 4:13). But the correct view in my eyes is that it is like raisin-cake raisins, and the proof is גפן שבמה, and the meaning is: moan, and you are stricken, when you remember the days of joy—the opposite of jubilation."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:54:00.956062319Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}